    "result_text": "Done. The build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## What I changed\n\nA single, cohesive addition to `OwnerCreatedEvent.emit(...)` in `src/main/java/.../util/OwnerCreatedEvent.java` \u2014 one statement emitting a line to the `NOTIFY` logger containing the owner id and the memberId:\n\n```java\nLoggerFactory.getLogger(\"NOTIFY\").info(\n    \"welcome owner id={} memberId={}\", owner.getId(), primaryIdentifier(owner));\n```\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\nThe create flow (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`) already calls `OwnerCreatedEvent.emit(owner, ...)` at the point of a successful save \u2014 after the id is assigned and `memberId` is set, only on the `CREATED` path. So the welcome notification belongs there semantically, and it's also by far the cheapest place under the cost model:\n\n- **`emit` lives in a tiny class**, so its `WMC_other` is ~2 (just `primaryIdentifier` + the private ctor). Adding the line to the large `addOwner` method instead would have paid the whole controller's WMC.\n- **CC stays 1** \u2014 no branching added; `emit` remains straight-line.\n- **One function changed, one file touched** \u2014 no new call sites, no new class, so the file multiplier is 1.\n\nThe test `AcceptanceTest.coreEnqueuesWelcome` now passes: `INFO NOTIFY - welcome owner id=11 memberId=NSW2616E6609E3`.",
